{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q9U6Y4","uniProtkbId":"GFPL2_ZOASP","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2004-12-21","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-06-10","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2000-05-01","entryVersion":67,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":4.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Zoanthus sp.","commonName":"Green polyp","taxonId":105402,"lineage":["Eukaryota","Metazoa","Cnidaria","Anthozoa","Hexacorallia","Zoantharia","Zoanthidae","Zoanthus"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"GFP-like fluorescent chromoprotein FP538"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"zFP538"}}],"contains":[{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"GFP-like fluorescent chromoprotein FP538 chain 1"}}},{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"GFP-like fluorescent chromoprotein FP538 chain 2"}}}]},"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10504696"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}],"value":"Pigment protein that is yellow in color"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES","absorption":{"max":528,"approximate":false,"note":{"texts":[{"value":"Exhibits a smaller absorbance peak at 494 nm. Has a strong fluorescence emission spectrum which peaks at 538 nm."}]},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}],"value":"Homotetramer"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10504696"}],"value":"Tentacle and oral disk"}],"commentType":"TISSUE SPECIFICITY"},{"texts":[{"value":"Contains a chromophore consisting of modified amino acid residues. The chromophore is formed by autocatalytic backbone condensation between Xaa-N and Gly-(N+2), and oxidation of Tyr-(N+1) to didehydrotyrosine. In addition, the residue N lysine undergoes cyclization. The alpha-amino nitrogen is replaced by the epsilon-amino nitrogen, the peptide chain is broken, residue N-1 is released as an amide, and a double bond is formed between the alpha-carbon and the nitrogen so that a tetrahydropyridine ring results. Maturation of the chromophore requires nothing other than molecular oxygen"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Fluorescent proteins have become a useful and ubiquitous tool for making chimeric proteins, where they function as a fluorescent protein tag. Typically they tolerate N- and C-terminal fusion to a broad variety of proteins. They have been expressed in most known cell types and are used as a noninvasive fluorescent marker in living cells and organisms. They enable a wide range of applications where they have functioned as a cell lineage tracer, reporter of gene expression, or as a measure of protein-protein interactions"}],"commentType":"BIOTECHNOLOGY"},{"texts":[{"value":"Fluorescence excitation of the Glu-66 mutant is at 493 nm and 550 nm with intense green emission at 405 nm and a weak red emission at 576 nm. Fluorescence emission of the Asp-66 mutant is at 524 nm and 552 nm and with a broad red emission shoulder extending from 650 nm"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10504696"}],"value":"Belongs to the GFP family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":65,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"GFP-like fluorescent chromoprotein FP538 chain 1","featureId":"PRO_0000010858"},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":231,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"GFP-like fluorescent chromoprotein FP538 chain 2","featureId":"PRO_0000010859"},{"type":"Site","location":{"start":{"value":65,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Cleavage"},{"type":"Modified residue","location":{"start":{"value":65,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":65,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Phenylalanine amide","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}]},{"type":"Modified residue","location":{"start":{"value":67,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":67,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"2,3-didehydrotyrosine","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"Q9U6Y8"}]},{"type":"Cross-link","location":{"start":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":68,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"2-tetrahydro-2-pyridyl-5-imidazolinone (Lys-Gly)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}]},{"type":"Mutagenesis","location":{"start":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Changes fluorescence emission from yellow to green.","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}],"alternativeSequence":{"originalSequence":"K","alternativeSequences":["A","C","F","G","H","L","N","S","T","P","Q"]}},{"type":"Mutagenesis","location":{"start":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Changes fluorescence emission from yellow to green with a weak red peak.","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}],"alternativeSequence":{"originalSequence":"K","alternativeSequences":["E","D"]}},{"type":"Mutagenesis","location":{"start":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":66,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Produces a non-fluorescent form.","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15628861"}],"alternativeSequence":{"originalSequence":"K","alternativeSequences":["I","R","W","Y"]}},{"type":"Beta strand","location":{"start":{"value":10,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":22,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"E